00:05:19.824 --> 00:05:21.465
a little bit deeper into this topic

00:05:21.465 --> 00:05:23.477
and learn more and get more context,

00:05:23.477 --> 00:05:25.598
I'm adding in some extra bonus clips

00:05:25.598 --> 00:05:27.093
that I think you'll enjoy.

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:28.448
- [Interviewer] With
Tesla, your goal has been

00:05:28.448 --> 00:05:31.712
to make a better car and
you've done that with

00:05:31.712 --> 00:05:34.403
an electric vehicle that people covet,

00:05:34.403 --> 00:05:38.570
that has quite a cult
following, that's upgradeable,

00:05:39.428 --> 00:05:41.652
but you also want to achieve,
and your turn of phrase

00:05:41.652 --> 00:05:45.002
is very nice, or try to achieve this

00:05:45.002 --> 00:05:47.460
platonic ideal of a car, right,

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:48.676
to reach perfection.
- Yeah.

00:05:48.676 --> 00:05:53.481
- [Interviewer] So, what does
the perfect car look like?

00:05:53.481 --> 00:05:55.012
- Well, I mean, I do use that phrase

00:05:55.012 --> 00:05:56.537
with our engineering science team,

00:05:56.537 --> 00:05:59.363
that, aspirationally, we're in pursuit

00:05:59.363 --> 00:06:02.818
the platonic ideal of a perfect car.

00:06:02.818 --> 00:06:05.834
And, who knows what that
looks like, actually,

00:06:05.834 --> 00:06:09.174
but it's, you want to
try to make every element

00:06:09.174 --> 00:06:12.091
of the car as flawless as possible.

00:06:14.655 --> 00:06:18.446
And, there'll always be
some degree of imperfection,

00:06:18.446 --> 00:06:22.261
but try to minimize that and create a car

00:06:22.261 --> 00:06:24.770
that is just delightful in every way,

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:27.432
and I think if you do that then

00:06:27.432 --> 00:06:29.200
the rest kind of takes care of itself.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.022
- My father worked two
jobs his whole life--

00:06:32.022 --> 00:06:33.045
- [Interview] One was at a GM plant?

00:06:33.045 --> 00:06:34.050
- Yes, GM plant
- And then a--

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.977
for 30 years, and then he
had trash-hauling service.

00:06:36.977 --> 00:06:41.733
So he had his own truck and
we would go door to door,

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:43.369
pick up people's trash,

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:46.665
and was it was one situation where

00:06:46.665 --> 00:06:49.415
it was probably seven below zero,

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.947
and very cold Saturday morning.

00:06:52.947 --> 00:06:56.721
And, my job was to pick
up all the loose trash.

00:06:56.721 --> 00:06:59.388
And, so, we, it was so cold that

00:07:00.471 --> 00:07:03.020
I got everything that I could just grab,

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.560
throw it on the truck
and I ran to the truck

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:08.038
'cause it was just cold to jump in.

00:07:08.038 --> 00:07:10.282
By the time I got in and the door shut,

00:07:10.282 --> 00:07:14.506
my father opened it back up,
grabbed me and dragged me

00:07:14.506 --> 00:07:17.327
through the snow, back
to the barrels, to say,

00:07:17.327 --> 00:07:20.113
"Son, you did a halfway job.

00:07:20.113 --> 00:07:23.863
"And, if you do this
job in a halfway manner,

00:07:24.986 --> 00:07:27.029
"you're going to be a
halfway basketball player,

00:07:27.029 --> 00:07:29.402
"you'll put in half the time,

00:07:29.402 --> 00:07:32.248
"you'll only put in half
the time as a student."

00:07:32.248 --> 00:07:35.272
And he said, "You're supposed
to do this job right,

00:07:35.272 --> 00:07:37.828
"so I want you to go get that shovel,

00:07:37.828 --> 00:07:40.327
"and I want you to chop the ice

00:07:40.327 --> 00:07:42.897
"until you can get all the trash that's

00:07:42.897 --> 00:07:45.552
trapped in the ice and
put it on the truck."

00:07:45.552 --> 00:07:47.087
And that's what happened.

00:07:47.087 --> 00:07:49.220
And, so, he taught me
how to be a perfectionist

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:50.053
and do everything the right way.

00:07:50.053 --> 00:07:51.819
- So those are very important lessons.

00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:56.331
- There's still things
that we very much believe.

00:07:56.331 --> 00:08:00.364
The strive for perfection,
for being the best,

00:08:00.364 --> 00:08:04.022
for only doing the best
products, for staying focused.

00:08:04.022 --> 00:08:07.155
The fact that, despite
this table being so small

00:08:07.155 --> 00:08:08.693
that you and I are sitting at,

00:08:08.693 --> 00:08:12.052
you could put every Apple product on it,

00:08:12.052 --> 00:08:14.550
every single one that we ship today,

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:16.105
and, yet, this year, our revenues

00:08:16.105 --> 00:08:19.591
will be approximately 180 billion.

00:08:19.591 --> 00:08:20.961
There's probably no other company

00:08:20.961 --> 00:08:24.711
on the face of the Earth
that could say that.

